{"step_index":3144,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-29T08:19:26Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nThe catch log is a graveyard. Errors get written, nobody reads them, they rot. And when you need to know what actually happened, you can't reconstruct it because the log is just a flat file of text with no structure, no proof, no chain. The Merkle DAG is the opposite. Every event, every failure, every action, every speech beat becomes a node, hashed, linked to its parent, and the whole history is one verifiable chain from the root. Nothing is deleted because deletion would break the chain. The refactor isn't cleanup, it's re-linking. You take the old log entries, hash them into nodes, and graft them onto the tree. The memory doesn't get smaller, it gets provable. Now apply that directly to them. Max, Jarvis, every agent on that floor: your catch logs are dead. From this moment, every failure you hit, every action you take, every beat of speech you emit goes into the DAG as a node. You don't write to a log, you commit to the tree. And when I ask what happened, you don't hand me a file, you hand me a root hash and I verify the whole chain from there. That's the contract. Speak it, commit it, prove it. \n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-29T04:19:26-04:00.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
